	        	<div id="350841733219030189-blog" class="blog-body" style="float: left;"> 
		<div id="wsite-content">	<div id="blog-post-579324304804402123" class="blog-post">
	
	
		<div class="blog-header">
			<h2 class="blog-title">
					<a class="blog-title-link blog-link" href="//www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/what-the-world-series-reveals-about-how-we-build-our-cities-and-communities-october-2025-repost">What the World Series Reveals About How We Build Our Cities and Communities (October 2025 Repost)</a>

			</h2>
			<p class="blog-date">
					<span class="date-text">
		12/11/2025
	</span>

			</p>
			<p class="blog-comments">
					<a href="//www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/what-the-world-series-reveals-about-how-we-build-our-cities-and-communities-october-2025-repost#comments" class="blog-link">
		0 Comments
	</a>

			</p>
		</div>
	
		<div class="blog-separator">&nbsp;</div>
	
		<div class="blog-content">
				<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/2d324276-9d7b-4e9d-bf46-b508c358a399-1789x1014_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">View: Left - Rogers Centre, photo by The Land of Dustin. Right - Dodger Stadium, photo via Pixabay.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br />&#8203;I love baseball. It is America&rsquo;s greatest pastime. Tonight is Game Two of the World Series, and if last night was any indication, we&rsquo;re in for a treat. If you are one of the lucky fans to have a ticket to a World Series game, you will encounter a different atmosphere, depending on which stadium you attend. I am not referring to the fan bases, but rather to what each ballpark and its surrounding area offer to the community.<br />&#8203;<br />Before and after the game tonight from the Rogers Centre (home of the Toronto Blue Jays), you can visit attractions such as the CN Tower, Ripley&rsquo;s Aquarium, the countless amount of bars, or even HTO Park on the Inner Harbour, all within a five-minute walk from where game two will be played, as well as the robust and reliable Toronto Subway.<br /><br /><span>The Blue Jays face the Los Angeles Dodgers, the&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/mlb/2025/04/02/2025-mlb-team-payrolls-highest-lowest/82751012007/">second-highest spending team in the MLB</a><span>. That fact is important when comparing the two communities these teams represent and play in.<br /><br />The Dodgers were previously located in Brooklyn, New York. They left in the mid-twentieth century for greater pastures in Los Angeles, chasing a new stadium and market. Los Angeles now ranks as the&nbsp;</span><a href="https://creators.yahoo.com/lifestyle/story/10-of-the-best-sports-cities-in-the-us-ranked-162844696.html">best sports market in the country</a><span>, with Dodger Stadium as the highest capacity stadium in the league at 56,000 seats.<br /></span><br />Yet, for all its size and history, Dodger Stadium stands largely isolated from the city it represents. Nestled in the hills of Chavez Ravine, it is surrounded by sprawling parking lots, a stark reminder of the communities that once lived there.<br /><br /></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a href='https://laist.com/news/la-history/dodger-stadium-chavez-ravine-battle' target='_blank'> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/1a0f5a97-d7fe-49bb-91fd-fbc3f98a5c3d-1460x1178_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">July 20, 1953: “Home owners from Chavez Ravine, Rose Hills, and Pacoima, tell Mayor Norris Poulson (left) to fight on for abandonment of housing projects.” Credit: (Herald-Examiner Collection/Los Angeles Public Library Collection, LAist).</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span>The stadium displaced the Mexican-American communities of La Loma, Palo Verde, and Bishop, forcing&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/dodgers-stadium-reparations-families-land-california-angeles-rcna145152">approximately 1,800 families to relocate</a><span>. They were told the land would soon host a new public housing project and that they could return when it was complete. That project never broke ground. Less than a decade later, the Dodgers arrived, and the empty land, which was once filled with homes, schools, and small businesses, was handed over for the construction of a stadium. The fight over that land became known as the&nbsp;</span><em><a href="https://laist.com/news/la-history/dodger-stadium-chavez-ravine-battle">Battle of Chavez Ravine</a></em><span>.<br /></span><br />Today, the Chavez Ravine community is unrecognizable. What once was a thriving neighborhood has been replaced by asphalt and concrete, surrounded by miles of empty parking lots that sit unused most days of the year. The area generates little local activity, offers few public amenities, and creates barriers instead of connections.<br /><br />That&rsquo;s the problem we find ourselves in today. The current model treats stadiums as destinations, not as parts of a living city, and in doing so, it leaves enormous economic and social potential untapped.<br />&#8203;<br />On the other side of the continent sits the City of Toronto in Canada, a community that shows up for its Blue Jays. Despite not being the most marketable city for sports or the highest spender of teams for baseball, the Blue Jays consistently punch above their weight, not only in on-the-field performance, but for creating a sense of community in downtown Toronto.</div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/5412f1e5-75fb-439f-90eb-efc522449694-2048x2048_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">View: Top - Toronto skyline, 2018. Bottom - Toronto skyline, 1990.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span>The last time the Toronto Blue Jays made the World Series in 1993, the city&rsquo;s population sat at just shy of 4.1 million. Today, that number is nearly&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.macrotrends.net/global-metrics/cities/20402/toronto/population">6.5 million</a><span>. That is a 58.54% increase in population for Toronto. The Rogers Centre and Blue Jays baseball isn&rsquo;t the sole reason why Toronto saw a surge in development over the past few decades, but rather, it suggests a pattern in smart growth planning from city officials.<br /><br /></span>Rogers Centre sits where old railyards used to be a half-century ago. That allowed prime development space for not only a new baseball stadium, but also restaurants, offices, bars, parks, and anything in between. With optimal proximity to public transportation (train, subway, and bus), the area saw demand for development in the early 2000s.<br /><span></span></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a href='https://alextabascio.wordpress.com/2019/06/12/torontos-zoning-by-law-diversity/' target='_blank'> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/4e1c3dc9-27ed-4487-b71d-7402bed3155a-2048x1448_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Figure: Zoning map of the City of Toronto (circa Alex Tabascio GIS).</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><span><br />It also helps that the area surrounding the Rogers Centre is zoned for mixed-use, allowing for more than one land use type on a plot of land. As a result, downtown Toronto became a community. In nature, it was a&nbsp;</span><a href="https://esl.uchicago.edu/2023/11/01/third-places-what-are-they-and-why-are-they-important-to-american-culture/">third place</a><span>&nbsp;for most residents who either lived or worked in the area. This is what happens when a ballpark or stadium is planned for the future.</span><br /><br />The City of Los Angeles, like many American cities in the mid-to-late 20th century, embraced a model of stadium building that prioritized cars over communities. From the 1960s through the 1980s, cities across the country built &ldquo;cookie-cutter&rdquo; multi-use stadiums. These were massive circular structures meant to host football, baseball, and concerts alike. While Dodger Stadium never adopted this model, it followed suit with many cities in prioritizing car-centric development for its stadiums. The nearest public transit stop is Chinatown Station, a 31-minute walk through endless parking from the stadium.<br /><br />They were efficient, practical, and brutally identical. Whether you were in Cincinnati, Pittsburgh, or Philadelphia, you would find nearly the same concrete bowl surrounded by endless parking, not even realizing which city you were in. Not thinking about the fan experience or the sense of community outside the game. That era has passed. Today&rsquo;s ballparks are no longer one-size-fits-all. They&rsquo;re increasingly designed to capture the fan experience: open concourses, local food vendors, team-specific architecture, and views that tie the game to the skyline.<br /><br />Now, many of these cookie-cutter stadiums no longer exist except for one, the Rogers Centre. It stands as a prime example of how to integrate a bad stadium concept into a community-oriented solution.<br /><br />We&rsquo;ve learned how to design for fans &mdash; now, we must learn to design for communities. Fortunately enough, that future isn&rsquo;t theoretical, but it&rsquo;s already emerging.<span><br /><br />In Minneapolis, U.S. Bank Stadium connects directly to light rail and spurred a wave of nearby development that has revitalized the city&rsquo;s East Town neighborhood. In suburban Atlanta, the Braves&rsquo; Truist Park redefined stadium planning entirely, integrating its urban core of commercial shops, bars, and restaurants within the stadium itself, in an area the city calls &ldquo;The Battery Atlanta.&rdquo; These are state-of-the-art stadiums built within the past ten years with new visions than their predecessors. The goal was fan experience on&nbsp;</span><em>and</em><span>&nbsp;off the field.</span><br /><br />Believe it or not, this stadium concept is not new. It vanished when cars hit the road, and the stadium experience off the field was deprioritized. Look at classic ballparks like Fenway Park in Boston, or Wrigley Field in Chicago. Both are at least a centurion old and still have some of the greatest fan experiences beyond the outfield walls. That&rsquo;s the thing, we lost our footing a long time ago with new stadiums. We forgot about the city and community these teams are supposed to represent and support.<span><br /><br />Stadiums, when done correctly, are powerful economic engines. They generate thousands of construction and long-term hospitality jobs, boost local spending on game days and beyond, and strengthen the local tax base through new development around the stadium. For example, a recent MIT and Northwestern University study found that a median sports facility yields roughly&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166046222000916?">$11.3 million in additional spending for local food, accommodation, and retail businesses</a><span>.</span><br /><br />That is serious economic growth, and yet, much of that potential is wasted when seas of asphalt parking lots surround a sports venue. This kind of stadium design is the difference between a family taking the subway in for the morning to visit shops, eat lunch, then hit an afternoon game, compared to one that will drive in and take no time to explore the community beyond the gates.<br /><br />As we watch the World Series unfold, it&rsquo;s worth remembering that stadiums are more than symbols of competition. They are reflections of our urban values. We can choose to build car-centric stadiums and communities, ones that negate the fan experience. Or, we can create a place where people want to be, utilizing the local economy. The next era of stadium design won&rsquo;t just determine how we experience sports, but rather, it will shape how we experience community itself.<br /><span></span></div>

		</div>
	
	
